    <!-- Troubleshooting & Common Gotchas -->
    <section class="rd-section" id="troubleshooting">
      <div class="rd-section-header">
        <div class="rd-section-badge">Troubleshooting Guide</div>
        <h2 class="rd-section-title">Common Gotchas & Solutions</h2>
        <p class="rd-section-desc">
          Running into issues during setup? Here are the top five configuration issues and how to resolve them quickly.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="rd-pitfalls-grid">
        <!-- Pitfall 1 -->
        <div class="rd-pitfall-card">
          <h3 class="rd-pitfall-title">Redirect URI Mismatch</h3>
          <p class="rd-pitfall-desc">
            Your Identity Provider returns an error like <em>"Invalid redirect_uri"</em> or <em>"redirect_uri_mismatch"</em>.
          </p>
          <div class="rd-pitfall-solution">
            <strong class="rd-solution-label">Solution:</strong> Ensure the redirect URI registered in your IdP console matches the exact URL of <code>oauth-receiver.html</code>, including protocol, port, and trailing path:
            <ul>
              <li>Exact port required: <code>http://localhost:3000/oauth-receiver.html</code></li>
              <li>Case sensitivity: <code>oauth-receiver.html</code> (all lowercase)</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Pitfall 2 -->
        <div class="rd-pitfall-card">
          <h3 class="rd-pitfall-title">CORS Error on Token Endpoint</h3>
          <p class="rd-pitfall-desc">
            The authorization code is acquired, but the browser console displays a CORS network failure when calling <code>tokenUrl</code>.
          </p>
          <div class="rd-pitfall-solution">
            <strong class="rd-solution-label">Solution:</strong> Because RapiDoc runs entirely in the browser, your IdP's <code>tokenUrl</code> must respond with standard CORS headers:
            <ul>
              <li><code class="mono-code">Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *</code> (or your docs origin)</li>
              <li>Ensure your client registration is set to <strong>SPA / Public Web</strong> in Auth0, Okta, or Azure AD.</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Pitfall 3 -->
        <div class="rd-pitfall-card">
          <h3 class="rd-pitfall-title">Popup Window Blocked</h3>
          <p class="rd-pitfall-desc">
            Clicking Authorize does not open the authentication window.
          </p>
          <div class="rd-pitfall-solution">
            <strong class="rd-solution-label">Solution:</strong> Browsers restrict popups that are not triggered by a direct user gesture:
            <ul>
              <li>Always trigger authorization by clicking the <strong>"Authorize"</strong> button directly.</li>
              <li>Check your browser address bar for popup blocking indicators and click "Always allow popups from this site".</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Pitfall 4 -->
        <div class="rd-pitfall-card">
          <h3 class="rd-pitfall-title">Client Secret Exposure</h3>
          <p class="rd-pitfall-desc">
            Committing client secrets into public OpenAPI specs compromises security.
          </p>
          <div class="rd-pitfall-solution">
            <strong class="rd-solution-label">Solution:</strong> Use PKCE for public documentation:
            <ul>
              <li>Add <code class="mono-code">x-pkce-only: true</code> under the flow definition.</li>
              <li>This removes the client secret input from the UI and relies on dynamic cryptographic code challenges (RFC 7636).</li>
              <li>Only use <code class="mono-code">x-client-secret</code> in internal mock or test sandboxes.</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Pitfall 5 -->
        <div class="rd-pitfall-card">
          <h3 class="rd-pitfall-title">Multiple Credentials / Auth Method Mismatch</h3>
          <p class="rd-pitfall-desc">
            Your Identity Provider (e.g. OpenIddict, Duende, Keycloak) returns <em>"Multiple client credentials cannot be specified"</em> or rejects token exchange.
          </p>
          <div class="rd-pitfall-solution">
            <strong class="rd-solution-label">Solution:</strong> RFC 6749 Section 2.3.1 mandates using only one client authentication method per token request:
            <ul>
              <li><strong>Default (Request Body):</strong> RapiDoc defaults to sending client credentials in the POST body (<code class="mono-code">client_secret_post</code>) to prevent browser CORS preflight failures.</li>
              <li><strong>Strict Basic Auth IdPs:</strong> If your server strictly requires HTTP Basic (<code class="mono-code">client_secret_basic</code>), add <code class="mono-code">x-receive-token-in: header</code> under your security scheme or select "Authorization Header" in the UI.</li>
              <li><strong>Public Clients (PKCE):</strong> For browser SPAs without a secret, use <code class="mono-code">x-pkce-only: true</code>; RapiDoc will transmit only <code class="mono-code">client_id</code> and <code class="mono-code">code_verifier</code> in the body without an Authorization header.</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

